## Economics, for context

Roughly $0.08–0.15 per connected minute all-in. At 50 calls/day that's about $5–6/day; at 300 calls/day about $30–35/day. One approved policy typically pays $500–1,000 commission, so calling cost is small relative to a single sale.

A cheaper VoIP vendor quoted me a much lower per-minute rate. I was advised to stay on Twilio/Retell initially — cheap routes risk poor audio, dropped calls, and C-level STIR/SHAKEN attestation, which gets caller IDs marked "Spam Likely". Answer rate matters far more than a fraction of a cent per minute at my volume. Revisit at 500+ calls/day.

## Legal framing I've already been told

AI-voice calls fall under the TCPA's rules for artificial/prerecorded voices, so they need prior express written consent for cell numbers, and consent given to a lead generator generally doesn't transfer to me. Purchased and aged lists are the risky case. I need documented consent per lead, monthly DNC refreshes, and an attorney's review before running a real campaign. Claude is not my lawyer and should keep saying so.
